🌐 Putting It All Together

Jab tum browser me example.com open karte ho, behind the scenes roughly ye hota hai:

Tumhara Browser
      ↓
DNS → Domain ka IP address find karta hai
      ↓
Web Server
      ↓
HTTP/HTTPS Request
      ↓
Server processing
      ↓
HTML + CSS + JS + Images
      ↓
Browser website display karta hai


Ab is process ko powerful banane ke liye kuch extra components use hote hain:

Load Balancer
CDN
Database
WAF
Web Server
Virtual Hosts
Backend
⚖️ 1. Load Balancer

Suppose ek website par bahut zyada users aa gaye.

Agar sirf ek server hai:

100,000 Users
      ↓
   Server


Server overload ho sakta hai.

Instead multiple servers:

              ┌── Server 1
Users → Load ├── Server 2
              └── Server 3


Load Balancer incoming requests ko different servers ke beech distribute karta hai.

Main benefits
1. Traffic distribution

Request ko different servers par distribute karta hai.

2. Failover

Agar Server 2 down hai:

Request → Load Balancer → Server 1 / Server 3


Load balancer Server 2 ko request bhejna temporarily stop kar dega.

🔄 Round Robin

Example:

Request 1 → Server 1
Request 2 → Server 2
Request 3 → Server 3
Request 4 → Server 1
Request 5 → Server 2


Turn-by-turn distribution.

⚖️ Weighted

Servers ki capacity different ho sakti hai.

Example:

Server 1 → powerful
Server 2 → medium
Server 3 → weak


Load balancer powerful server ko comparatively zyada traffic de sakta hai.

❤️ Health Check

Load balancer periodically check karta hai:

"Server alive hai?"

Agar server response nahi karta → traffic us server ko nahi bhejega.

🌍 2. CDN

CDN = Content Delivery Network

CDN ka purpose mainly static files ko users ke closer rakhna hai.

Static files:

Images
CSS
JavaScript
Videos
etc.

Without CDN:

India User
    ↓
Website Server → USA


Data ko long distance travel karna padega.

CDN ke saath:

India User
    ↓
Nearby CDN Server
    ↓
Image/CSS/JS


Result:

✅ Faster loading
✅ Original server par less traffic
✅ Better performance globally

Easy analogy

Imagine ek restaurant ka main kitchen USA me hai.

Agar India ke har customer ka food USA se aaye → slow.

CDN = different cities me small kitchens/food warehouses bana dena.

🗄️ 3. Database

Website ko information store karni hoti hai.

For example:

Username
Password
Email
Posts
Comments
Orders


Ye information database me store ho sakti hai.

Common databases:

MySQL
MSSQL
MongoDB
PostgreSQL

Basic architecture:

User
 ↓
Web Server
 ↓
Backend
 ↓
Database


Example:

Tum login karte ho:

Username: alice
Password: ********


Backend database se check karega:

Kya Alice ka account exist karta hai aur credentials correct hain?

Then response:

Correct → Login successful
Wrong   → Login failed

🛡️ 4. WAF

WAF = Web Application Firewall

WAF website ke front gate security guard jaisa hai.

Architecture:

User
 ↓
WAF
 ↓
Web Server
 ↓
Backend


WAF incoming HTTP requests inspect karta hai.

Agar request suspicious lage:

Attacker → WAF → ❌ BLOCK


Normal request:

User → WAF → ✅ Web Server


WAF common attack patterns detect karne ki koshish kar sakta hai.

Rate Limiting

WAF ye bhi check kar sakta hai ki koi IP bahut zyada requests to nahi bhej raha.

Example:

Normal:
10 requests/sec → ✅

Suspicious:
10,000 requests/sec → 🚫


Ye excessive traffic/DoS-type situations ko mitigate karne me help kar sakta hai.

🖥️ 5. Web Server

Web server = software jo incoming web requests receive karta hai aur web content return karta hai.

Common examples:

Apache
Nginx
Node.js-based servers

Basic flow:

Browser
   ↓
HTTP Request
   ↓
Web Server
   ↓
Requested file/content
   ↓
HTTP Response

📁 Web Root Directory

Web server ke paas ek directory hoti hai jahan se files serve hoti hain.

Example:

/var/www/html/


Agar browser request kare:

http://example.com/picture.jpg


Server internally file dhundh sakta hai:

/var/www/html/picture.jpg


Aur us file ko browser ko return karega.

🏠 6. Virtual Hosts

Ek hi web server par multiple websites host kar sakte ho.

Example:

                 Web Server
               /     |      \
              /      |       \
        one.com   two.com   three.com


Browser request me hostname aata hai:

Host: one.com


Web server check karta hai:

one.com ke liye kaunsa website configuration hai?

Then correct website serve karta hai.

Example:

one.com
   ↓
/var/www/website_one

two.com
   ↓
/var/www/website_two

Important

One physical server → multiple websites

Ye possible hai because of Virtual Hosts.

📄 Static vs Dynamic Content

Ye bahut important distinction hai.

🟢 Static Content

Jo content normally same rehta hai.

Examples:

Images
CSS
JavaScript files
Fixed HTML

Example:

/logo.png
/style.css
/script.js


Server directly file serve kar sakta hai.

Browser → Server → File

🔵 Dynamic Content

Jo request/user/data ke according change ho sakta hai.

Example:

Blog

Aaj:

Latest Post:
"Networking Basics"


Kal new post add hua:

Latest Post:
"Web Security"


Homepage automatically different content show karegi.

Another example:

Search: cats


Result:

10 cat-related results


Search:

Search: dogs


Result:

15 dog-related results


Ye dynamic content hai.

🧠 Frontend vs Backend

Simple trick:

Frontend

Jo tum dekh sakte ho.

HTML
CSS
JavaScript


Browser me execute/render hota hai.

Backend

Jo server ke peeche hota hai.

Backend:

data process karta hai
database se communicate karta hai
login handle karta hai
calculations karta hai
response generate karta hai

User generally backend ka actual source code nahi dekhta.

🐘 Backend Languages

Examples:

PHP
Python
Ruby
Node.js
Perl

Ye backend languages databases aur external services ke saath interact kar sakti hain.

🧪 PHP Example

Suppose URL hai:

example.com/index.php?name=adam


Backend PHP code:

<html>
<body>
Hello <?php echo $_GET["name"]; ?>
</body>
</html>


Yahan:

?name=adam


means name ki value = adam.

Backend PHP execute karega aur output banega:

<html>
<body>
Hello adam
</body>
</html>


Browser ko PHP code nahi milta.

Browser ko sirf final HTML milti hai:

Server-side PHP
      ↓
Processing
      ↓
Final HTML
      ↓
Browser

🔥 Cybersecurity point

Backend user input process karta hai, isliye agar developer input ko properly validate/sanitize nahi karta, to vulnerabilities arise ho sakti hain.

